WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:10.510
Narrator: Burned Louis Vuitton bags,

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.713
chewed-up designer heels,
damaged Chanel handbags,

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:15.715
and worn-out Gucci sneakers:

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:18.118
these accessories have seen better days.

00:00:18.118 --> 00:00:22.989
We follow professionals as
they restore nine luxury items.

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:27.227
Our first expert is Rio Jongsae Kim,

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:29.629
from Kim's Shoe &amp; Bag Repair.

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:33.633
He shows us how he mends
these $1,000 Gucci mules,

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:35.635
making them look brand new.

00:16:08.267 --> 00:16:11.003
[cloth rustling]

00:16:13.238 --> 00:16:16.041
[brush scratching]

00:16:16.041 --> 00:16:17.543
I am Charlotte Staerck,

00:16:17.543 --> 00:16:20.846
and I'm cofounder and
CEO of Handbag Clinic.

00:16:20.846 --> 00:16:22.448
Today, I'm going to walk you through

00:16:22.448 --> 00:16:24.283
how our artisan Chloe

00:16:24.283 --> 00:16:26.685
will restore this Grand Shopper Tote

00:16:26.685 --> 00:16:29.088
that has pen ink all over it.

00:16:29.088 --> 00:16:30.789
The Chanel Grand Shopper Tote

00:16:30.789 --> 00:16:33.592
reached around 2,000 pounds

00:16:33.592 --> 00:16:36.762
when it was able to be
bought direct from Chanel.

00:16:36.762 --> 00:16:38.764
When this restoration's complete,

00:16:38.764 --> 00:16:41.100
we would sell a Grand Shopper Tote

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:44.169
for anywhere between 1,700 pounds

00:16:44.169 --> 00:16:47.239
and 1,950 pounds.

00:16:52.911 --> 00:16:55.614
The first thing that Chloe
will do with this item

00:16:55.614 --> 00:16:58.617
is remove all of the ink.

00:16:58.617 --> 00:17:01.620
The most important part
of restoring this bag

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:05.257
will actually be the initial cleaning,

00:17:05.257 --> 00:17:08.560
because ink can spread and
can create more staining.

00:17:11.296 --> 00:17:13.665
We use a special ink remover.

00:17:13.665 --> 00:17:15.467
So, you can see Chloe

00:17:15.467 --> 00:17:18.937
taking the ink remover
with a small cotton bud

00:17:18.937 --> 00:17:22.908
and gently rolling this
over the initial ink.

00:17:22.908 --> 00:17:25.344
This is so that we don't
have any spreading,

00:17:25.344 --> 00:17:29.214
any further contamination
of that ink to the bag.

00:17:29.214 --> 00:17:31.316
One of the main reasons for this

00:17:31.316 --> 00:17:34.186
is because ink is actually very oily,

00:17:34.186 --> 00:17:36.655
and oil and leather really don't mix.

00:17:36.655 --> 00:17:39.992
[cotton bud scratching]

00:17:45.964 --> 00:17:48.767
We then also use a range of products.

00:17:48.767 --> 00:17:50.536
Some are solvent-based,

00:17:50.536 --> 00:17:53.072
which will help to dry out any areas

00:17:53.072 --> 00:17:55.541
which have absorbed additional oils.

00:17:59.611 --> 00:18:01.914
And then we also use products

00:18:01.914 --> 00:18:05.217
which have natural oils
embedded into them,

00:18:05.217 --> 00:18:08.954
which will then nourish
areas that have dried,

00:18:08.954 --> 00:18:12.891
such as the corners or any areas

00:18:12.891 --> 00:18:15.194
where the ink has been drawn out of.

00:18:17.729 --> 00:18:20.399
[brush rasping]

00:18:22.101 --> 00:18:25.671
We then use our specialist
cleaning solution

00:18:25.671 --> 00:18:28.173
with the brush and a foam,

00:18:28.173 --> 00:18:32.177
which then will remove any excess product.

00:18:32.177 --> 00:18:34.413
Caviar skin leather is grained,

00:18:34.413 --> 00:18:37.416
so you need the brush to get
into all of those grooves

00:18:37.416 --> 00:18:39.384
to be able to really make sure it's clean

00:18:39.384 --> 00:18:42.287
and make sure it's ready for
the next stage of the process.

00:18:42.287 --> 00:18:44.690
Caviar leather, it gets its name

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:47.459
from being a pebbled-texture leather,

00:18:47.459 --> 00:18:50.729
which means that it has printed into it

00:18:50.729 --> 00:18:53.265
this almost bubbled effect.

00:18:53.265 --> 00:18:55.968
If anything is trapped
there or left in there,

00:18:55.968 --> 00:19:00.305
you would run the risk of that
later peeling or coming away.

00:19:01.273 --> 00:19:04.276
[airbrush hissing]

00:19:04.276 --> 00:19:06.845
Because it is ink that
has contaminated this,

00:19:06.845 --> 00:19:10.182
the cleaning process alone
will not remove this stain,

00:19:10.182 --> 00:19:12.751
and we must apply other methods.

00:19:12.751 --> 00:19:14.353
[airbrush hissing]

00:19:14.353 --> 00:19:16.488
In our research and development team,

00:19:16.488 --> 00:19:19.291
we realized that ink
stains, in particular,

00:19:19.291 --> 00:19:21.059
were very stubborn,

00:19:21.059 --> 00:19:23.996
so we created a metallic solution

00:19:23.996 --> 00:19:26.298
which actually blocks that coming through

00:19:26.298 --> 00:19:28.433
to make sure in a year's time

00:19:28.433 --> 00:19:29.668
you're not going to start seeing

00:19:29.668 --> 00:19:31.370
that ink stain come through.

00:19:33.205 --> 00:19:35.107
Chloe takes the specialist solution

00:19:35.107 --> 00:19:37.976
and she applies it
lightly with an airbrush

00:19:37.976 --> 00:19:41.246
throughout every area
where you can see the ink.

00:19:41.246 --> 00:19:42.714
[airbrush hissing]

00:19:45.617 --> 00:19:47.986
Chloe will then create the color match,

00:19:47.986 --> 00:19:51.557
which we have discussed
with the client in advance.

00:19:51.557 --> 00:19:54.993
[palette knife scraping]

00:19:56.762 --> 00:19:58.697
This colorant, once created,

00:19:58.697 --> 00:20:01.700
will then be airbrushed on this bag.

00:20:07.439 --> 00:20:10.275
We always want to be
as sparing as possible

00:20:10.275 --> 00:20:13.512
to ensure the texture of
the leather is maintained.

00:20:14.413 --> 00:20:17.282
Because of the iconic CC quilting

00:20:17.282 --> 00:20:20.052
and the CC on the front of the item,

00:20:20.052 --> 00:20:23.589
which makes this item
the iconic Chanel look,

00:20:23.589 --> 00:20:24.823
we want to make sure

00:20:24.823 --> 00:20:28.927
as little pigment gets onto
those stitches as possible.

00:20:28.927 --> 00:20:30.495
[airbrush hissing]

00:20:30.495 --> 00:20:32.864
And the bag is ready to go.

00:21:09.334 --> 00:21:11.103
My name is Artem Amirov.

00:21:11.103 --> 00:21:15.274
I'm the owner of Royal
Gloss Restoration Studio

00:21:15.274 --> 00:21:16.642
here in Russia,

00:21:16.642 --> 00:21:19.845
and I'm the head restoration artist there.

00:21:19.845 --> 00:21:21.747
Today, I'm going to walk you through

00:21:21.747 --> 00:21:26.051
how we restored this $42,000
Celine crocodile bag.

00:21:26.051 --> 00:21:28.654
It was made around eight years ago,

00:21:28.654 --> 00:21:31.723
and they were producing
it for one year only.

00:21:31.723 --> 00:21:33.225
This makes it really rare.

00:21:33.225 --> 00:21:35.861
There was a huge amount of problems on it.

00:21:35.861 --> 00:21:38.597
There was scuffs and
scratches all over the bag.

00:21:38.597 --> 00:21:42.034
The lining inside the bag
was completely messed up.

00:21:42.034 --> 00:21:45.837
The bag was missing the
puller for the zipper.

00:21:51.677 --> 00:21:54.613
We start with removing the edge paint.

00:21:54.613 --> 00:21:56.081
[grinder whirring]

00:21:56.081 --> 00:21:59.017
All the edges of the bag
have scuffs and scratches.

00:22:00.085 --> 00:22:02.621
We use the grinder to sand it down.

00:22:06.458 --> 00:22:09.127
We begin with disassembling the bag.

00:22:10.829 --> 00:22:14.232
The inner lining had a lot
of scratches from active use.

00:22:14.232 --> 00:22:16.835
As well, it had some dirt stains.

00:22:16.835 --> 00:22:18.503
To clean and fix the inner lining,

00:22:18.503 --> 00:22:20.906
we had to pull apart all the bag.

00:22:24.042 --> 00:22:26.845
The tools that we used were a seam ripper

00:22:26.845 --> 00:22:28.814
and a cobbler knife.

00:22:28.814 --> 00:22:30.515
The seam ripper is more preferred

00:22:30.515 --> 00:22:32.417
for delicate parts of the bag.

00:22:35.053 --> 00:22:37.422
We carefully slide under the stitch

00:22:37.422 --> 00:22:40.092
and pull the stitch upward to get it out.

00:22:46.298 --> 00:22:48.834
The original lining for
Celine crocodile bag

00:22:48.834 --> 00:22:50.202
is made of sheepskin.

00:22:51.069 --> 00:22:53.739
We also use sheepskin
with the same quality,

00:22:53.739 --> 00:22:56.541
with the same color,
and the same softness.

00:22:57.376 --> 00:23:00.612
After we carefully took off
all the sheepskin linings

00:23:00.612 --> 00:23:01.813
from each side of the bag,

00:23:01.813 --> 00:23:03.915
we replaced them with the new ones.

00:23:06.218 --> 00:23:09.921
To cut the new lining out,
we use a rotary cutter.

00:23:14.493 --> 00:23:18.397
The glue that we use is the
same that the manufacturer use.

00:23:21.266 --> 00:23:23.502
It has a very strong bonding.

00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:30.742
In order to make a new zipper for our bag,

00:23:30.742 --> 00:23:32.944
we use the new zipper.

00:23:34.012 --> 00:23:35.814
We use new leather.

00:23:35.814 --> 00:23:38.717
We choose a pattern to
match the original scales,

00:23:38.717 --> 00:23:41.720
and we paint it the same color as the bag.

00:23:50.362 --> 00:23:51.830
In order to clean the handbag,

00:23:51.830 --> 00:23:56.635
we use a special leather
shampoo and a semisoft brush.

00:24:02.474 --> 00:24:04.643
When we talk about cleaning
the crocodile skin,

00:24:04.643 --> 00:24:07.279
it is difficult because
you can damage the patina;

00:24:07.279 --> 00:24:11.016
even with a mild cleaner,
you can just wipe it out.

00:24:13.652 --> 00:24:16.087
One of the biggest concerns is the scales,

00:24:16.087 --> 00:24:18.490
which cracked on the handle.

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:20.492
If it's not prevented in time,

00:24:20.492 --> 00:24:23.495
later it will be very
difficult to repair them.

00:24:25.664 --> 00:24:27.766
It's also had greasy stains on it,

00:24:27.766 --> 00:24:29.968
so in order to remove the stains

00:24:29.968 --> 00:24:33.371
and for our paint to
penetrate through the handle

00:24:33.371 --> 00:24:36.308
and to have a bond with the surface,

00:24:36.308 --> 00:24:38.310
we need to use acetone

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:40.479
to remove the greasy stains.

00:24:42.514 --> 00:24:45.016
Applying an edge paint
coat is essential part

00:24:45.016 --> 00:24:47.886
to keeping all the edges
sealed and prevent the bag

00:24:47.886 --> 00:24:50.255
from further moisture penetration.

00:24:52.624 --> 00:24:56.761
To apply the edge paint, we
use a special roller pen.

00:24:56.761 --> 00:24:59.264
It grabs a certain amount of edge paint

00:24:59.264 --> 00:25:02.534
and allows us to allocate it to the edges.

00:25:06.071 --> 00:25:07.339
In order to color match,

00:25:07.339 --> 00:25:10.976
I'm testing colors on separate
crocodile leather skin.

00:25:14.913 --> 00:25:17.682
I'm trying to mimic the fading

00:25:17.682 --> 00:25:20.385
that goes from the center to the edges,

00:25:20.385 --> 00:25:24.089
from blue to dark blue
and then to the black.

00:25:24.089 --> 00:25:26.625
It gives the bag a very unique look.

00:25:30.295 --> 00:25:32.030
In order to apply the paint,

00:25:32.030 --> 00:25:35.567
I'm using airbrush and small spray gun.

00:25:36.668 --> 00:25:38.169
Working with them allows me

00:25:38.169 --> 00:25:41.339
to accurately distribute
paint all over the surface.

00:25:45.877 --> 00:25:47.913
We use water-based paints,

00:25:47.913 --> 00:25:50.515
which will cover up all the defects.

00:25:52.384 --> 00:25:55.687
These kinds of paints have
great adhesion properties.

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:07.866
We had two types of sewing machines

00:26:07.866 --> 00:26:10.635
to get the same old stitch holes.

00:26:10.635 --> 00:26:13.738
We regulate the length between the holes.

00:26:15.740 --> 00:26:18.109
One of the sewing machines
was primarily used

00:26:18.109 --> 00:26:20.478
to sew the thickest part of the leather.

00:26:25.183 --> 00:26:26.918
We had to be careful with

00:26:26.918 --> 00:26:28.954
assembling the bag back together,

00:26:28.954 --> 00:26:31.556
because while assembling
all the sides of the bag,

00:26:31.556 --> 00:26:34.492
we had to consider the
thickness of the leather layers,

00:26:34.492 --> 00:26:37.596
so they won't move during
the sewing process.

00:26:38.563 --> 00:26:40.565
For some parts, we did a hand-sewing

00:26:40.565 --> 00:26:43.368
to avoid damaging the crocodile leather.

00:27:33.418 --> 00:27:35.053
My name is Tom Rago,

00:27:35.053 --> 00:27:36.488
and I am the co-owner

00:27:36.488 --> 00:27:39.124
of Rago Brothers Shoe &amp; Leather Repair

00:27:39.124 --> 00:27:41.593
with my brother, Tony Rago.

00:27:41.593 --> 00:27:44.195
And today I am going to walk you through

00:27:44.195 --> 00:27:48.033
a dog-chewed Louboutin shoe.

00:27:48.033 --> 00:27:52.671
Approximate cost is $800 to $1,000.

00:27:52.671 --> 00:27:53.905
When they came in to us,

00:27:53.905 --> 00:27:58.076
the platform was halfway chewed off,

00:27:58.076 --> 00:28:01.146
the top of the shoe wasn't there,

00:28:01.146 --> 00:28:04.049
and the heels were all chewed,

00:28:04.049 --> 00:28:05.583
all had bite marks on it.

00:28:05.583 --> 00:28:08.787
And you would think that this
was an unrepairable shoe.

00:28:08.787 --> 00:28:12.223
We are going to attempt to rebuild

00:28:12.223 --> 00:28:13.792
the platform of the shoe,

00:28:13.792 --> 00:28:16.261
the upper part, the black patent leather,

00:28:16.261 --> 00:28:18.897
and get the structure back to the shoe.

00:28:18.897 --> 00:28:21.800
[machine whirring]

00:28:23.802 --> 00:28:26.071
To start out the process,

00:28:26.071 --> 00:28:29.140
we are going to give this
to our repairman, Ronnie,

00:28:29.140 --> 00:28:32.610
who is going to put this shoe together.

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:34.446
[heat gun blowing]

00:28:34.446 --> 00:28:37.048
We are using the heat gun
to remove the platform

00:28:37.048 --> 00:28:39.551
very carefully, trying to loosen the glue

00:28:39.551 --> 00:28:42.587
between the shoe and the platform.

00:28:45.924 --> 00:28:49.594
We now are putting the
platform down on the table

00:28:49.594 --> 00:28:52.197
and taking a piece of bonded leather

00:28:52.197 --> 00:28:54.299
and tracing out the platform

00:28:54.299 --> 00:28:57.135
so that we can make a
mold for the other shoe.

00:28:58.236 --> 00:29:00.905
[machine whirs]

00:29:04.042 --> 00:29:06.644
What that involved is
flipping the platform over

00:29:06.644 --> 00:29:07.946
so it looked like a left shoe

00:29:07.946 --> 00:29:09.280
instead of the right shoe.

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:10.982
So we did that.

00:29:10.982 --> 00:29:12.350
We took our measurements.

00:29:23.728 --> 00:29:25.096
Once we've got that pattern,

00:29:25.096 --> 00:29:27.999
we now are taking a hard piece of rubber

00:29:27.999 --> 00:29:30.168
and we are shaping that with a Dremel

00:29:30.168 --> 00:29:32.370
to match the other platform.

00:29:35.373 --> 00:29:37.842
The platform, when you
get it from Louboutin,

00:29:37.842 --> 00:29:40.512
it's a molded plastic base.

00:29:42.514 --> 00:29:44.682
Molded plastic is not available to us,

00:29:44.682 --> 00:29:49.254
so we cut it out of a very
rigid piece of rubber,

00:29:49.254 --> 00:29:51.689
and we had to sand it to make

00:29:51.689 --> 00:29:54.292
the exact curves that it needed to have

00:29:54.292 --> 00:29:56.227
to fit the upper part of the shoe.

00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:04.836
When you start with the
upper part of the shoe,

00:30:04.836 --> 00:30:07.005
you take a flat piece of patent leather

00:30:07.005 --> 00:30:09.073
and you take the inside material,

00:30:09.073 --> 00:30:11.209
which is a bonded leather --

00:30:11.209 --> 00:30:12.477
that gives it the structure.

00:30:12.477 --> 00:30:13.878
That gives it the form.

00:30:15.947 --> 00:30:18.650
[heat gun blows]

00:30:18.650 --> 00:30:19.651
What we're doing now is we're

00:30:19.651 --> 00:30:22.453
heating up the leather on the platform

00:30:22.453 --> 00:30:23.955
so that we can stretch it,

00:30:23.955 --> 00:30:25.623
make it more malleable

00:30:25.623 --> 00:30:28.626
so that it has no creases in it.

00:30:28.626 --> 00:30:32.096
And then we pull it and
attach it to the platform.

00:30:35.099 --> 00:30:37.869
So, now we have the top shape of the shoe.

00:30:39.571 --> 00:30:40.939
After we've gotten that,

00:30:40.939 --> 00:30:45.243
we begin to form it to
the platform underneath.

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:52.183
[scissors snipping]

00:30:54.853 --> 00:30:56.588
We have to mold that platform

00:30:56.588 --> 00:30:57.622
around the top of the shoe,

00:30:57.622 --> 00:31:00.692
and that requires us using a hand Dremel

00:31:00.692 --> 00:31:02.827
to get the shape of the platform

00:31:02.827 --> 00:31:05.530
to fit onto the upper part of the shoe.

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:07.031
That's just trial and error.

00:31:07.031 --> 00:31:09.300
You may go through that several times.

00:31:13.371 --> 00:31:15.039
So, now that we've repaired

00:31:15.039 --> 00:31:16.674
the upper part of the shoe,

00:31:16.674 --> 00:31:18.376
we're going to take the platform

00:31:18.376 --> 00:31:20.178
and attach the platform

00:31:20.178 --> 00:31:21.980
to the upper part of the shoe.

00:31:25.617 --> 00:31:28.086
Now we are going to give the shoe

00:31:28.086 --> 00:31:30.588
to our shoe repairman, Henry,

00:31:30.588 --> 00:31:34.025
so that he can attach the
leather and rubber soles

00:31:34.025 --> 00:31:35.693
to the bottom of the shoe.

00:31:35.693 --> 00:31:37.262
[hammering]

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:43.268
Now what we're doing is

00:31:43.268 --> 00:31:44.969
we're attaching the red sole

00:31:44.969 --> 00:31:46.671
onto the bottom of the shoe.

00:31:52.176 --> 00:31:54.312
We are going to take this shoe,

00:31:54.312 --> 00:31:57.315
and we're going to edge
it with black paint,

00:31:57.315 --> 00:31:59.284
so that from the side of the shoe

00:31:59.284 --> 00:32:01.853
all you see is the black.

00:32:01.853 --> 00:32:03.688
You won't see any red.

00:32:14.966 --> 00:32:17.068
This shoe was really a challenge for us,

00:32:17.068 --> 00:32:19.304
and we think it came out really great.

00:32:19.304 --> 00:32:21.005
We think that when the owner

00:32:21.005 --> 00:32:22.540
does get this shoe back,

00:32:22.540 --> 00:32:24.375
her mouth is gonna drop.

00:32:29.047 --> 00:32:31.182
[ripping]

00:32:34.752 --> 00:32:37.488
[scraping]

00:32:40.058 --> 00:32:41.326
My name is Gerry Gallagher,

00:32:41.326 --> 00:32:42.527
master leather craftsman

00:32:42.527 --> 00:32:44.395
and founder of Leather Surgeons.

00:32:44.395 --> 00:32:47.131
Today we have a black
caviar Chanel classic flap

00:32:47.131 --> 00:32:50.268
jumbo bag that's been severely damaged

00:32:50.268 --> 00:32:51.803
by an incandescent light bulb.

00:32:51.803 --> 00:32:53.604
We had this customer reach out to us.

00:32:53.604 --> 00:32:56.474
She did a closet restoration, actually,

00:32:56.474 --> 00:32:59.644
and somehow an incandescent light bulb

00:32:59.644 --> 00:33:01.312
from a contractor burned its way

00:33:01.312 --> 00:33:03.081
clear through her handbag.

00:33:03.081 --> 00:33:05.316
Through the outer flap,
through the lining.

00:33:05.316 --> 00:33:06.751
But what I'm going to try to do here

00:33:06.751 --> 00:33:10.621
is just graft new leather
over the damaged area

00:33:10.621 --> 00:33:12.123
and preserve as much of the original

00:33:12.123 --> 00:33:14.292
part of the bag as I possibly can.

00:33:14.292 --> 00:33:17.061
[chain clicking]

00:33:20.932 --> 00:33:23.701
I'm going to start by
opening up all the stitches

00:33:23.701 --> 00:33:26.204
around the front flap of the bag.

00:33:27.271 --> 00:33:29.474
[ripping]

00:33:36.514 --> 00:33:38.216
We're going to use a sewing awl

00:33:38.216 --> 00:33:40.551
and a really sharp knife

00:33:40.551 --> 00:33:43.121
to be able to just get in between

00:33:43.121 --> 00:33:44.956
the lining of the bag
and the shell of the bag

00:33:44.956 --> 00:33:46.758
without causing any damage.

00:33:46.758 --> 00:33:49.560
I like to pull out the
stitches one at a time,

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:51.896
'cause eventually when we
stitch this bag back together,

00:33:51.896 --> 00:33:53.831
we're going to have to stitch
in the same exact holes

00:33:53.831 --> 00:33:56.267
that the manufacturer
stitched in the first time.

00:33:59.971 --> 00:34:03.474
[ripping]

00:34:03.474 --> 00:34:06.277
I'm going to open up the two gusset tacks

00:34:06.277 --> 00:34:10.148
so that we can access the
interior lining of the bag,

00:34:10.148 --> 00:34:12.150
between the shell and
the lining of the bag,

00:34:12.150 --> 00:34:13.918
and see just how much we need to do

00:34:13.918 --> 00:34:15.887
as far as replacing the internals.

00:34:18.389 --> 00:34:20.958
[ripping]

00:34:22.593 --> 00:34:25.229
The burn went completely
through the outer shell

00:34:25.229 --> 00:34:27.665
and through the interior
lining of the bag.

00:34:28.833 --> 00:34:31.402
[wiping]

00:34:40.445 --> 00:34:43.047
[scraping]

00:34:43.047 --> 00:34:45.283
For the most challenging part of this job,

00:34:45.283 --> 00:34:48.753
we have the damaged area in
the lower right quadrant.

00:34:48.753 --> 00:34:50.621
So it will be much easier for me

00:34:50.621 --> 00:34:52.056
to take the front panel off

00:34:52.056 --> 00:34:53.825
and just recreate the front panel.

00:34:53.825 --> 00:34:55.726
But what I'd like to do
is preserve as much of

00:34:55.726 --> 00:34:57.862
the front panel of the
bag as I possibly can

00:34:57.862 --> 00:35:00.798
and just replace the part that's damaged.

00:35:00.798 --> 00:35:02.266
So I'm going take my
knife and I'm going to

00:35:02.266 --> 00:35:05.870
very carefully skive off,
which means cut away,

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:08.339
the existing leather
that's burned or damaged.

00:35:13.211 --> 00:35:14.879
I'm going to make our little pattern,

00:35:14.879 --> 00:35:17.915
and I'm going to replace
that piece of leather.

00:35:30.228 --> 00:35:33.030
I use a pattern to cut the
size of the front panel

00:35:33.030 --> 00:35:35.333
and make sure it's 100% accurate.

00:35:49.747 --> 00:35:52.049
Because the bag was so severely damaged

00:35:52.049 --> 00:35:54.318
and it was burned completely through,

00:35:54.318 --> 00:35:57.355
and actually clear through
the interior lining,

00:35:57.355 --> 00:36:00.358
I'm going to cut fresh lining panels.

00:36:03.494 --> 00:36:05.196
We have patterns already precut

00:36:05.196 --> 00:36:06.998
for this particular bag.

00:36:06.998 --> 00:36:09.233
The interior lining of
the bag is made of goat,

00:36:09.233 --> 00:36:12.270
so we replace it with goat.

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:15.573
As far as Chanel, this bag
is Chanel caviar leather,

00:36:15.573 --> 00:36:17.008
which we also stock.

00:36:18.142 --> 00:36:21.078
We're able to replace
everything about this bag

00:36:21.078 --> 00:36:23.247
exactly the way it was
done the first time.

00:36:28.986 --> 00:36:31.289
I'm going to take that
piece of caviar leather

00:36:31.289 --> 00:36:32.757
and I'm going to hand-skive it,

00:36:32.757 --> 00:36:34.492
which means shave it down.

00:36:42.066 --> 00:36:44.168
If I skive the leather paper-thin

00:36:44.168 --> 00:36:46.237
and I lay it right along that line,

00:36:46.237 --> 00:36:49.140
when I drop my new stitch over that seam,

00:36:49.140 --> 00:36:51.442
there'll be zero transition between

00:36:51.442 --> 00:36:53.711
what was there before
and what we have now.

00:36:54.645 --> 00:36:56.714
It should completely disappear.

00:37:05.923 --> 00:37:08.759
I'm gonna lie and cement
that piece of leather

00:37:08.759 --> 00:37:10.361
right over that seam.

00:37:11.796 --> 00:37:14.832
This a traditional leather contact cement,

00:37:14.832 --> 00:37:17.902
so you need to apply the
cement to both surfaces

00:37:17.902 --> 00:37:19.770
and give it a minute or two to set

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:22.473
and then press the pieces together.

00:37:38.189 --> 00:37:40.925
[sewing machine clicking]

00:37:42.793 --> 00:37:44.061
When you're doing a restoration

00:37:44.061 --> 00:37:45.763
and you want to put the stitch back in

00:37:45.763 --> 00:37:47.832
the stitch hole that was there before,

00:37:47.832 --> 00:37:49.867
you need to aim the needle for the hole.

00:37:49.867 --> 00:37:52.770
So when I requilt this leather,

00:37:52.770 --> 00:37:54.505
any stitches that are visible,

00:37:54.505 --> 00:37:55.906
I have to make sure I drop the stitch

00:37:55.906 --> 00:37:57.174
in the same exact hole.

00:37:58.709 --> 00:38:02.046
[sewing machine clicking]

00:38:03.781 --> 00:38:06.717
It's just a question of
measuring everything out.

00:38:06.717 --> 00:38:09.553
Because the flap quilting has to match

00:38:09.553 --> 00:38:11.088
the body of the bag quilting.

00:38:11.088 --> 00:38:12.223
When you close that bag,

00:38:12.223 --> 00:38:15.259
all of those seams should line up.

00:38:15.259 --> 00:38:18.596
[sewing machine clicking]

00:38:20.464 --> 00:38:22.900
Now we're going to unweave
the leather of the chain.

00:38:22.900 --> 00:38:25.236
This customer felt that
the patina on the gold

00:38:25.236 --> 00:38:26.871
was starting to fade a little bit,

00:38:26.871 --> 00:38:29.106
so we replate the metal.

00:38:36.647 --> 00:38:39.950
Then I'm going to reweave
that leather through the chain

00:38:39.950 --> 00:38:41.952
and stitch the two ends.

00:39:03.474 --> 00:39:05.976
I feel really good about
what we did here today.

00:39:05.976 --> 00:39:08.979
We took a bag that would've
otherwise been trashed,

00:39:08.979 --> 00:39:10.981
and we gave it a new lease on life.

00:39:21.325 --> 00:39:24.595
I'm Taleen Akopyan,
cofounder of Purse Rehab.

00:39:24.595 --> 00:39:26.497
We are a designer-handbag- and

00:39:26.497 --> 00:39:28.699
leather-goods-restoration company.

00:39:28.699 --> 00:39:30.301
Today, I'm going to walk you through

00:39:30.301 --> 00:39:31.769
how my team restores

00:39:31.769 --> 00:39:34.438
a burned Louis Vuitton Neverfull bag.

00:39:34.438 --> 00:39:38.008
This is the Damier Ebene Neverfull print.

00:39:38.008 --> 00:39:40.277
It's their top-selling bag today.

00:39:40.277 --> 00:39:44.448
This bag is about $2,000,
depending on the size.

00:39:44.448 --> 00:39:47.618
Unfortunately, the bag
was left, by accident,

00:39:47.618 --> 00:39:50.121
on a burning electric stovetop.

00:39:50.121 --> 00:39:53.457
So the entire base is basically burned,

00:39:53.457 --> 00:39:55.192
along with the piping,

00:39:55.192 --> 00:39:57.328
and the side of the bag is also burned.

00:40:03.067 --> 00:40:05.369
To start, we're going
to unstitch the base.

00:40:05.369 --> 00:40:06.537
And in order to do that,

00:40:06.537 --> 00:40:10.040
we're using a round awl
to remove the stitching

00:40:10.040 --> 00:40:12.543
and remove the piping as well.

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:18.582
We're going to clean off
some of the debris here

00:40:18.582 --> 00:40:21.118
using a soft-bristled brush.

00:40:21.118 --> 00:40:23.254
This is a coated canvas bag.

00:40:23.254 --> 00:40:25.990
Basically, coated canvas is cotton

00:40:25.990 --> 00:40:28.359
that's coated with polyvinyl chloride.

00:40:28.359 --> 00:40:30.828
So that's why you have that black color

00:40:30.828 --> 00:40:33.230
and the hardness of the canvas.

00:40:34.231 --> 00:40:37.701
Now we are drawing the template

00:40:37.701 --> 00:40:40.438
using the base that we removed.

00:40:42.740 --> 00:40:45.142
And we are using cowhide leather.

00:40:45.142 --> 00:40:46.377
This is premium leather

00:40:46.377 --> 00:40:49.947
that matches the existing trim on the bag.

00:40:49.947 --> 00:40:53.951
We're going to also skive
the base at the edges.

00:40:53.951 --> 00:40:56.954
Skiving is essentially
shaving down the leather,

00:40:56.954 --> 00:40:58.622
and it's usually done at the edges

00:40:58.622 --> 00:41:02.059
so that they're easily
foldable or stitchable.

00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:07.765
To match the interior
red canvas of the bag,

00:41:07.765 --> 00:41:11.602
we are cutting a new piece of red material

00:41:11.602 --> 00:41:14.438
to glue on and stitch onto the base

00:41:14.438 --> 00:41:17.641
so that the interior is fully compatible

00:41:17.641 --> 00:41:19.410
with the existing lining.

00:41:19.410 --> 00:41:22.746
And it's very much like
the Louis Vuitton material.

00:41:25.616 --> 00:41:29.353
We have to make a new
piping to add to the base.

00:41:29.353 --> 00:41:31.655
This is made out of the same leather.

00:41:33.357 --> 00:41:38.229
And so we cut out the piping
and glue it onto the base.

00:41:45.302 --> 00:41:47.404
And, in addition to gluing it,

00:41:47.404 --> 00:41:49.907
we will also be stitching the piping

00:41:49.907 --> 00:41:52.109
so that it's double reinforcement.

00:41:52.109 --> 00:41:54.211
Simply gluing it isn't going to give it

00:41:54.211 --> 00:41:56.347
that extra strength that it needs,

00:41:56.347 --> 00:42:00.050
because it is a base, so it's
a stress point on the bag.

00:42:02.453 --> 00:42:04.288
In addition to making the base,

00:42:04.288 --> 00:42:07.758
we are going to recreate the burned piece

00:42:07.758 --> 00:42:09.326
that's on the side of the bag.

00:42:09.326 --> 00:42:12.796
We are using recycled
Louis Vuitton material

00:42:12.796 --> 00:42:14.632
that's in the Damier Ebene print,

00:42:14.632 --> 00:42:16.634
so that it looks more seamless

00:42:16.634 --> 00:42:19.103
and it blends in nicely with the bag.

00:42:20.337 --> 00:42:24.608
We try to align the pattern
on the bag first visually,

00:42:24.608 --> 00:42:27.745
to make sure that we can make it fit.

00:42:27.745 --> 00:42:30.014
Once we cut it, we are going to glue it

00:42:30.014 --> 00:42:33.784
onto the canvas and also stitch it.

00:42:35.486 --> 00:42:39.290
And once the recycled piece is on the bag,

00:42:39.290 --> 00:42:42.960
we're going to trim off
the excess burned portion

00:42:42.960 --> 00:42:45.129
that's underneath the bag.

00:42:49.567 --> 00:42:53.270
Once we've completed the
preparation of the new base,

00:42:53.270 --> 00:42:55.005
it's time to put it on the bag.

00:42:55.005 --> 00:42:58.909
First, we glue it onto the
bag using leather glue,

00:42:58.909 --> 00:43:02.313
and then we are stapling the base

00:43:02.313 --> 00:43:04.081
onto the body of the bag.

00:43:04.081 --> 00:43:07.451
It's a unique feature that
our craftsmen use here

00:43:07.451 --> 00:43:09.720
so that when we're stitching it together,

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:11.722
it's solid and it doesn't move.

00:43:12.690 --> 00:43:16.193
Once we're done stitching
the base onto the bag,

00:43:16.193 --> 00:43:18.262
we are removing the staples

00:43:18.262 --> 00:43:20.931
using a round-tipped staple remover,

00:43:20.931 --> 00:43:24.068
which does not damage the
canvas or the leather.

00:43:31.041 --> 00:43:33.911
Once we're done with the structural issues

00:43:33.911 --> 00:43:35.179
and the repairs on the bag,

00:43:35.179 --> 00:43:38.115
we're cleaning it using a mild cleaner

00:43:38.115 --> 00:43:41.185
to revitalize and condition the canvas.

00:43:46.123 --> 00:43:47.958
It would've been impossible for us

00:43:47.958 --> 00:43:51.528
to match the exact same
pattern and material.

00:43:51.528 --> 00:43:54.999
We did our best to come up with a solution

00:43:54.999 --> 00:43:57.267
that does blend well with the bag

00:43:57.267 --> 00:43:59.770
and makes it usable in the long run.

00:43:59.770 --> 00:44:02.473
And that, essentially,
was our client's goal.

00:44:13.751 --> 00:44:16.453
My name's Freya Bass, and I own Bagsamoré,

00:44:16.453 --> 00:44:20.090
a handbag-restoration
company in Sydney, Australia.

00:44:20.090 --> 00:44:21.258
Today, I'm going to walk you through

00:44:21.258 --> 00:44:25.162
how I restore a $4,000 Chanel vanity case.

00:44:25.162 --> 00:44:26.563
Typical of a bag of this age,

00:44:26.563 --> 00:44:29.967
the lining is disintegrating,
as it is a coated fabric.

00:44:29.967 --> 00:44:33.237
We also have wear and
tear to the outer corners.

00:44:37.941 --> 00:44:42.146
This bag was purchased
10 years ago for $1,500.

00:44:42.146 --> 00:44:46.517
It is today worth between
$3,000 and $5,000,

00:44:46.517 --> 00:44:48.185
so in its restored state,

00:44:48.185 --> 00:44:51.455
it will be worth more towards $5,000.

00:44:51.455 --> 00:44:52.356
To begin the process,

00:44:52.356 --> 00:44:54.925
I'm going to remove
the strap from the bag.

00:44:54.925 --> 00:44:57.027
So I'm going to do this
using some small pliers

00:44:57.027 --> 00:45:00.064
to gently remove the chain from the link.

00:45:00.064 --> 00:45:01.365
The strap is very worn.

00:45:01.365 --> 00:45:04.268
So there is color wear, dirt, buildup,

00:45:04.268 --> 00:45:06.203
and the metalwork is tarnished.

00:45:06.203 --> 00:45:08.906
I'm then going to take a quick unpick

00:45:08.906 --> 00:45:12.342
and undo the stitches
at the end of the chain.

00:45:12.342 --> 00:45:15.646
I now need to unthread the
leather through the chain

00:45:15.646 --> 00:45:18.782
so that it's completely
separated from the metalwork.

00:45:28.559 --> 00:45:31.195
Now I've fully removed the
leather from the chain,

00:45:31.195 --> 00:45:33.363
I'm going to clean the strap.

00:45:34.331 --> 00:45:36.100
So the leather strap will just be cleaned

00:45:36.100 --> 00:45:38.502
initially with foam cleaner.

00:45:38.502 --> 00:45:41.171
I'm doing this with a
horsehair hide brush,

00:45:41.171 --> 00:45:42.272
which is nice and gentle

00:45:42.272 --> 00:45:44.341
to get into the grain of the leather.

00:45:47.945 --> 00:45:49.747
And then I'm going to
continue this process

00:45:49.747 --> 00:45:51.949
throughout the exterior of the bag.

00:45:51.949 --> 00:45:55.252
So I'm just applying foam
cleaner to the bag's surface,

00:45:55.252 --> 00:45:56.854
doing one panel at a time.

00:45:57.755 --> 00:46:00.824
I'm gently massaging the foam
into the grain of the leather.

00:46:04.061 --> 00:46:05.763
It's always important to clean the bag

00:46:05.763 --> 00:46:07.698
before applying pigment.

00:46:07.698 --> 00:46:09.133
This will mean that the pigment

00:46:09.133 --> 00:46:10.968
will bond to the leather better.

00:46:10.968 --> 00:46:13.103
If there's anything left
on the surface of the bag,

00:46:13.103 --> 00:46:15.773
such as silicones from protector sprays

00:46:15.773 --> 00:46:17.574
or day-to-day dirt buildup,

00:46:17.574 --> 00:46:20.444
this could hinder the bonding process.

00:46:29.453 --> 00:46:33.056
Now I'm going to clean
the interior of the bag.

00:46:33.056 --> 00:46:36.927
I just need to clean as much
of the debris away as possible.

00:46:36.927 --> 00:46:39.997
So I'm using a much more
gentle cleaner here,

00:46:39.997 --> 00:46:42.366
which is like a luxury leather cleaner.

00:46:42.366 --> 00:46:44.168
So it's a water-based cleaner,

00:46:44.168 --> 00:46:46.136
and I'm basically just
going to gently wipe

00:46:46.136 --> 00:46:47.771
inside the lining.

00:46:49.706 --> 00:46:51.175
This has to be done really gently,

00:46:51.175 --> 00:46:53.777
as if any pressure is put on

00:46:53.777 --> 00:46:56.280
or if a product that
was incorrect was used,

00:46:56.280 --> 00:46:58.982
it will then start to remove
even more of the lining.

00:47:00.050 --> 00:47:00.918
We need to preserve

00:47:00.918 --> 00:47:02.986
as much of the original lining as possible

00:47:02.986 --> 00:47:05.622
to make the job easier
in the restoring stage.

00:47:10.828 --> 00:47:13.197
Now we've fully cleaned
the bag inside and out,

00:47:13.197 --> 00:47:17.034
I'm going to move over
to the repair process.

00:47:17.034 --> 00:47:19.636
The interior of the bag is disintegrating.

00:47:19.636 --> 00:47:21.438
This is quite common with this age bag,

00:47:21.438 --> 00:47:24.241
as it was made from a coated fabric.

00:47:24.241 --> 00:47:26.877
Over time and due to humidity,

00:47:26.877 --> 00:47:28.245
the surface of the lining

00:47:28.245 --> 00:47:30.447
will start to crumble and come away.

00:47:30.447 --> 00:47:32.082
We need to restore the lining

00:47:32.082 --> 00:47:33.817
as close to the original as possible,

00:47:33.817 --> 00:47:35.652
so we're going to rebuild the surface

00:47:35.652 --> 00:47:37.387
using fillers and pigments.

00:47:38.388 --> 00:47:40.924
So here I'm going to
use a flexible filler.

00:47:40.924 --> 00:47:44.561
This filler moves with the
surface of the leather or fabric.

00:47:44.561 --> 00:47:45.729
So I'm just going to apply this

00:47:45.729 --> 00:47:48.131
using a very soft and small paintbrush

00:47:48.131 --> 00:47:50.834
so I can get a nice even surface

00:47:50.834 --> 00:47:53.303
across the whole interior lining.

00:47:53.303 --> 00:47:55.806
The idea is to restore the original

00:47:55.806 --> 00:47:58.508
and avoid having to replace
something like this.

00:47:58.508 --> 00:48:01.945
This means we retain the
original Chanel logo.

00:48:01.945 --> 00:48:04.047
I'm going to do this
very slowly to make sure

00:48:04.047 --> 00:48:07.150
that I get a good even
coverage all the way around.

00:48:07.150 --> 00:48:10.087
I'm going to start using
the filler along the top rim

00:48:10.087 --> 00:48:11.788
and then work my way down

00:48:11.788 --> 00:48:14.491
into the main crevices of the lining.

00:48:14.491 --> 00:48:18.528
Once applied, this filler
needs 24 hours to cure.

00:48:18.528 --> 00:48:21.298
Now I've left the filler for 24 hours,

00:48:21.298 --> 00:48:23.667
I'm sanding down the surface.

00:48:23.667 --> 00:48:26.770
This is to remove any
sort of bits of grain

00:48:26.770 --> 00:48:30.374
or lumps or bumps and try
and get a smooth finish.

00:48:30.374 --> 00:48:31.475
I'm just going to start sanding

00:48:31.475 --> 00:48:33.277
around the top really gently.

00:48:33.277 --> 00:48:35.545
I'm using a very fine sandpaper

00:48:35.545 --> 00:48:37.547
so it doesn't grab or wear down

00:48:37.547 --> 00:48:40.350
the surface of the original lining.

00:48:40.350 --> 00:48:44.321
And I'm then going to wipe
over with some alcohol.

00:48:44.321 --> 00:48:46.456
This will remove any of the surface dust

00:48:46.456 --> 00:48:49.326
that has been created from the sandpaper,

00:48:49.326 --> 00:48:51.461
and then it will also
slightly soften the filler

00:48:51.461 --> 00:48:52.829
to help it smooth

00:48:52.829 --> 00:48:56.066
and blend into the original
surface of the lining.

00:49:04.041 --> 00:49:05.442
Before airbrushing, I'm just going to

00:49:05.442 --> 00:49:08.111
remove the CC lock hardware.

00:49:08.111 --> 00:49:09.413
This makes it much easier

00:49:09.413 --> 00:49:11.815
to get an even coat around the lock.

00:49:11.815 --> 00:49:13.450
I'm just going to unscrew this

00:49:13.450 --> 00:49:15.953
with a small flat-head screwdriver.

00:49:15.953 --> 00:49:16.987
Once I've removed that,

00:49:16.987 --> 00:49:20.324
I'm going to tape up the
remaining hardware on the bag.

00:49:24.394 --> 00:49:25.629
So now I'm just going to move on

00:49:25.629 --> 00:49:27.864
to mixing a color for the bag.

00:49:27.864 --> 00:49:30.200
I custom-mix every color by eye,

00:49:30.200 --> 00:49:32.636
so I tend to use about
three or four paints

00:49:32.636 --> 00:49:33.470
to mix each color.

00:49:33.470 --> 00:49:38.308
So here I'm using caramel,
gold yellow, and citrine.

00:49:38.308 --> 00:49:39.643
I mix these together,

00:49:39.643 --> 00:49:41.111
and I'm just going to test the color

00:49:41.111 --> 00:49:44.014
on a small area on the bag
to make sure it matches.

00:49:44.014 --> 00:49:46.183
This can take few attempts sometimes

00:49:46.183 --> 00:49:49.086
just to make sure that the
color is absolutely spot on.

00:49:51.655 --> 00:49:53.724
Now I've got my color, and I'm good to go.

00:49:53.724 --> 00:49:56.293
I'm just going to pop it into an airbrush.

00:49:56.293 --> 00:49:58.528
I use the airbrush, as it allows me

00:49:58.528 --> 00:50:01.999
to create lots of fine coats of pigment

00:50:01.999 --> 00:50:06.169
rather than painting it on,
which would go on too thick.

00:50:06.169 --> 00:50:09.406
It's really important not
to too-heavy-coat a bag

00:50:09.406 --> 00:50:11.775
with paints, pigments, and fillers.

00:50:11.775 --> 00:50:13.043
Less is always more.

00:50:13.043 --> 00:50:15.512
So we still want to preserve the original

00:50:15.512 --> 00:50:18.648
look and feel of the bag.

00:50:18.648 --> 00:50:20.517
I'm just going to work my way

00:50:20.517 --> 00:50:22.052
round each section of the bag,

00:50:22.052 --> 00:50:24.421
concentrating on the
areas that have more wear.

00:50:25.389 --> 00:50:29.192
Now I've managed to get
a nice coat of pigment

00:50:29.192 --> 00:50:30.193
over all of the bag,

00:50:30.193 --> 00:50:32.729
I'm then going to use a finish.

00:50:32.729 --> 00:50:34.631
A finish is a clear-coat sealant

00:50:34.631 --> 00:50:36.400
which goes on top of the pigment

00:50:36.400 --> 00:50:38.869
to protect it from wear and tear.

00:50:38.869 --> 00:50:41.471
Here I've mixed about
two-thirds high gloss

00:50:41.471 --> 00:50:44.775
to a third matte to
get a nice satin sheen,

00:50:44.775 --> 00:50:47.177
which reflects the original leather.

00:50:48.111 --> 00:50:52.716
I'm just going to airbrush
this finish over the whole bag

00:50:52.716 --> 00:50:54.651
and then leave it to dry.

00:50:54.651 --> 00:50:56.853
Now moving on to the leather in the strap,

00:50:56.853 --> 00:50:59.222
I'm actually going to paint
this on with a paintbrush

00:50:59.222 --> 00:51:01.591
so as not to have too much wasted paint.

00:51:01.591 --> 00:51:03.527
So I'm just going to run

00:51:03.527 --> 00:51:05.695
all the way along the strap with one coat,

00:51:05.695 --> 00:51:08.665
leave it to dry, and
then repeat the process.

00:51:08.665 --> 00:51:10.100
I'm then going to finish this off,

00:51:10.100 --> 00:51:11.902
again, with airbrushing the finish

00:51:11.902 --> 00:51:14.671
for a nice even layer
across the whole strap.

00:51:16.406 --> 00:51:18.675
Now I'm going to move on to the lining.

00:51:18.675 --> 00:51:22.179
I'm going to take my
paintbrush and paint in

00:51:22.179 --> 00:51:26.283
over all of the areas where
the surface had chipped away.

00:51:26.283 --> 00:51:27.851
This may take a few coats,

00:51:27.851 --> 00:51:29.953
as it was showing white underneath,

00:51:29.953 --> 00:51:31.588
but I need to rebuild the color back up

00:51:31.588 --> 00:51:33.523
to match the surface lining.

00:51:45.535 --> 00:51:48.672
Once I've done that, I'm
going to apply a finish.

00:51:48.672 --> 00:51:51.508
Due to the severity of the
condition of this lining,

00:51:51.508 --> 00:51:53.877
there will still remain
a few imperfections,

00:51:53.877 --> 00:51:57.647
but what we've done is we
sealed and recoated the surface,

00:51:57.647 --> 00:52:00.750
so this will prevent any
further damage from happening

00:52:00.750 --> 00:52:03.353
and give this handbag a new lease on life.

00:52:08.258 --> 00:52:10.527
Now that I've completed
all of the airbrushing,

00:52:10.527 --> 00:52:13.930
I am now going to polish the hardware.

00:52:13.930 --> 00:52:16.266
Here I'm going to use a
fine-grade metal polish

00:52:16.266 --> 00:52:18.768
to just gently polish
the surface of the metal.

00:52:18.768 --> 00:52:21.004
This'll remove any of the tarnishing.

00:52:21.004 --> 00:52:24.407
As I'm doing so, you
can see the dark color

00:52:24.407 --> 00:52:27.377
of the tarnished metalwork
coming off onto the cotton bud.

00:52:28.445 --> 00:52:32.115
I'm going to polish all
of the links on this strap

00:52:32.115 --> 00:52:34.584
to bring them up to a nice shiny gold.

00:52:37.187 --> 00:52:38.588
Now all the color work is done,

00:52:38.588 --> 00:52:41.358
I'm going to reattach the strap.

00:52:41.358 --> 00:52:42.792
I'm going to, firstly,

00:52:42.792 --> 00:52:45.028
re-thread the leather through the strap.

00:52:45.028 --> 00:52:47.764
It's really important that you
line the leather up properly

00:52:47.764 --> 00:52:50.534
so that you get the same amount each end.

00:52:50.534 --> 00:52:52.002
I am now using the pliers

00:52:52.002 --> 00:52:55.805
to reattach the chain to the bag.

00:52:55.805 --> 00:52:58.241
Now I need to stitch the
leather back together

00:52:58.241 --> 00:52:59.910
through the original holes.

00:52:59.910 --> 00:53:01.645
I'm taking some matching-color thread

00:53:01.645 --> 00:53:03.013
and using a very fine needle

00:53:03.013 --> 00:53:05.515
so I can get through the original holes.

00:53:05.515 --> 00:53:08.018
It's really important to go
through the original holes,

00:53:08.018 --> 00:53:09.886
as if you make a new hole in leather,

00:53:09.886 --> 00:53:11.922
there's no way of reversing that

00:53:11.922 --> 00:53:14.958
without then going through
another repair process.

00:53:14.958 --> 00:53:17.661
I'm just going to secure
with a couple of stitches

00:53:17.661 --> 00:53:20.263
and then tie it in on
itself to keep it neat.

00:53:21.498 --> 00:53:23.733
And now I just need to thread the end

00:53:23.733 --> 00:53:24.901
back through the leather

00:53:24.901 --> 00:53:26.836
and then secure it with
a little bit of glue,

00:53:26.836 --> 00:53:28.872
just like it was originally done.

00:53:30.874 --> 00:53:32.842
And now I'm going to
apply a leather protector

00:53:32.842 --> 00:53:34.311
to the surface of the leather.

00:53:34.311 --> 00:53:38.348
This will protect the bag
from dye transfer from jeans,

00:53:38.348 --> 00:53:42.152
UV damage, and liquid and oil staining.

00:53:42.152 --> 00:53:44.354
It's really important to
protect your leather bags

00:53:44.354 --> 00:53:46.156
for future wear and tear.

00:53:50.827 --> 00:53:53.997
I'm really happy how the
restoration of this bag turned out.

00:53:53.997 --> 00:53:56.600
The interior is now ready to be worn,

00:53:56.600 --> 00:53:58.468
and the lining is all fixed up.

00:53:58.468 --> 00:54:01.204
The outside is no longer showing
any signs of wear and tear,

00:54:01.204 --> 00:54:03.707
and the gold hardware is
looking nice and shiny.

00:54:03.707 --> 00:54:04.808
I think my customer is going to be

00:54:04.808 --> 00:54:06.343
really pleased with the outcome.

